WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.425
Welcome back to Building Azure Security.

2
00:00:02.425 --> 00:00:05.800
This is Chapter 4: the
web application firewall.

3
00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:09.438
A firewall in detection mode
is a log with good intentions.

4
00:00:09.438 --> 00:00:10.835
The check reads the mode,

5
00:00:10.835 --> 00:00:14.326
and the switch to prevention
stays a decision a person makes.

6
00:00:14.326 --> 00:00:18.357
We'll start from what Microsoft Learn
gives you, see what it leaves to you,

7
00:00:18.357 --> 00:00:22.457
read how the kit checks every policy
and every internet-facing endpoint,

8
00:00:22.457 --> 00:00:25.737
and why it writes the switch to
a plan instead of sending it.

9
00:00:25.737 --> 00:00:29.088
Then two labs, the
evidence and the dangers.

10
00:00:29.088 --> 00:00:34.120
The chapter has been reviewed and
approved, and the code is illustrative.

11
00:00:37.870 --> 00:00:39.733
Why does the mode matter so much?

12
00:00:39.733 --> 00:00:42.106
Because, as Microsoft Learn puts it,

13
00:00:42.106 --> 00:00:46.033
detection mode only logs, and
only prevention mode blocks.

14
00:00:46.033 --> 00:00:51.120
By the end of this chapter you can check
every WAF policy against Microsoft's Zero

15
00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:56.663
Trust recommendations, find every internet-facing
application gateway and Front Door

16
00:00:56.663 --> 00:01:01.295
profile that has no WAF or no logs,
compute the guide's prevention coverage,

17
00:01:01.295 --> 00:01:05.091
and hand the application owner
the exact command that moves

18
00:01:05.091 --> 00:01:08.508
a policy to prevention, without
the kit ever sending it.

19
00:01:08.508 --> 00:01:11.940
It's for application owners
who tune and switch the WAF,

20
00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:17.170
platform owners who own the policies,
and security monitors who read the logs.

21
00:01:20.920 --> 00:01:22.795
Here's Microsoft's starting point.

22
00:01:22.795 --> 00:01:27.890
Microsoft's Zero Trust recommendations
for both services check prevention mode,

23
00:01:27.890 --> 00:01:32.460
request body inspection, the default
rule set, the bot protection rule set,

24
00:01:32.460 --> 00:01:35.682
rate limiting and diagnostic
logging, among others.

25
00:01:35.682 --> 00:01:40.626
Learn recommends the latest Default Rule
Set, two point two at the guide's reading,

26
00:01:40.626 --> 00:01:45.569
validated in log mode before blocking; the
command line's default is two point one.

27
00:01:45.569 --> 00:01:48.358
Front Door's managed rules
need the Premium tier.

28
00:01:48.358 --> 00:01:53.046
Built-in policies require a WAF on
each service, and a given mode.

29
00:01:53.046 --> 00:01:58.180
And Learn documents the switch between
detection and prevention with one command.

30
00:02:01.930 --> 00:02:03.293
What does Learn leave to you?

31
00:02:03.293 --> 00:02:07.322
Its Validate query reads one
policy; an estate has several,

32
00:02:07.322 --> 00:02:11.518
on two services, so the kit reads
them all with the same checks.

33
00:02:11.518 --> 00:02:16.761
Front Door says body inspection is enabled
where Application Gateway says true,

34
00:02:16.761 --> 00:02:21.692
and code written for one reads the other
as off, so each shape is read as its own.

35
00:02:21.692 --> 00:02:24.994
A policy that requires a
WAF audits resources but

36
00:02:24.994 --> 00:02:28.054
doesn't count endpoints,
so the kit counts them.

37
00:02:28.054 --> 00:02:33.305
A Front Door's WAF lives in a security
policy, so those are read per profile.

38
00:02:33.305 --> 00:02:36.302
The switch to prevention,
sent by automation,

39
00:02:36.302 --> 00:02:39.705
blocks users before tuning,
so it's written to a plan.

40
00:02:39.705 --> 00:02:44.560
And an old rule set is a warning,
because moving needs validation first.

41
00:02:48.310 --> 00:02:49.710
Eight rules come out of that.

42
00:02:49.710 --> 00:02:53.015
A policy in detection, or
disabled, is a finding,

43
00:02:53.015 --> 00:02:55.798
and so is body inspection switched off.

44
00:02:55.798 --> 00:03:01.260
A missing or older rule set, no bot
protection, and no rate limit are warnings.

45
00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:06.686
An internet-facing gateway or Front Door
profile with no WAF at all is a finding,

46
00:03:06.686 --> 00:03:10.610
and so is one that sends no
logs to the platform workspace.

47
00:03:10.610 --> 00:03:14.334
And a Front Door policy that
isn't Premium is information:

48
00:03:14.334 --> 00:03:16.235
it can't use managed rule sets.

49
00:03:16.235 --> 00:03:19.801
Coverage is the endpoints
behind a WAF in prevention

50
00:03:19.801 --> 00:03:23.448
over all internet-facing
endpoints, the guide's metric.

51
00:03:23.448 --> 00:03:27.250
A gateway with only a private
frontend isn't counted.

52
00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:34.000
Here's the one thing this check
could change, and doesn't.

53
00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:39.012
For every Application Gateway policy not
in prevention, the check writes Learn's

54
00:03:39.012 --> 00:03:43.950
switch command, with the policy's resource
group and subscription, to a plan file.

55
00:03:43.950 --> 00:03:47.662
For a Front Door policy it writes
a line pointing at the guide.

56
00:03:47.662 --> 00:03:48.912
Nothing runs either.

57
00:03:48.912 --> 00:03:53.605
A person runs the command after the
false-positive review, in a change window,

58
00:03:53.605 --> 00:03:58.299
because a switch sent by automation
before tuning blocks legitimate users.

59
00:03:58.299 --> 00:04:02.350
Learn documents the same command
in reverse for backing out.

60
00:04:06.100 --> 00:04:07.150
Who's involved?

61
00:04:07.150 --> 00:04:10.735
The application owner tunes the
policy while it's in detection,

62
00:04:10.735 --> 00:04:13.700
and runs the switch after
the false-positive review.

63
00:04:13.700 --> 00:04:17.825
The platform owner owns the policies
and their rule-set versions.

64
00:04:17.825 --> 00:04:20.620
And the security monitor
reads the WAF logs,

65
00:04:20.620 --> 00:04:24.812
and confirms every endpoint's logs
reach the platform workspace.

66
00:04:24.812 --> 00:04:26.937
The kit's reads need only Reader.

67
00:04:26.937 --> 00:04:29.890
The plan is the
application owner's to run.

68
00:04:33.640 --> 00:04:37.752
Lab 1 needs PowerShell 7 with
Pester, in the kit's folder.

69
00:04:37.752 --> 00:04:42.392
Contoso has two WAF policies: one
on its portal application gateway,

70
00:04:42.392 --> 00:04:45.127
and one on its portal Front Door profile.

71
00:04:45.127 --> 00:04:48.677
A second Front Door profile,
for careers, has none.

72
00:04:48.677 --> 00:04:52.853
Step 1 runs the check: one of
three internet-facing endpoints

73
00:04:52.853 --> 00:04:56.778
is behind a WAF in prevention,
and the plan file is written.

74
00:04:56.778 --> 00:04:59.041
Step 2 reads each policy.

75
00:04:59.041 --> 00:05:01.828
The gateway's policy
is still in detection.

76
00:05:01.828 --> 00:05:05.353
The Front Door policy blocks,
but has no bot rule set.

77
00:05:05.353 --> 00:05:08.350
Both are on Default
Rule Set two point one.

78
00:05:12.100 --> 00:05:14.350
Step 3 reads the findings.

79
00:05:14.350 --> 00:05:16.863
The gateway's policy is in detection.

80
00:05:16.863 --> 00:05:21.375
The careers Front Door profile
has no WAF and sends no logs.

81
00:05:21.375 --> 00:05:24.852
Both policies are a rule-set
version behind the baseline,

82
00:05:24.852 --> 00:05:27.737
and the Front Door policy
has no bot protection.

83
00:05:27.737 --> 00:05:32.163
The intranet gateway has a private
frontend only, so it isn't counted.

84
00:05:32.163 --> 00:05:35.098
Step 4 reads the plan the
kit wrote and didn't run:

85
00:05:35.098 --> 00:05:38.034
Learn's switch command
for the gateway's policy,

86
00:05:38.034 --> 00:05:42.700
with its resource group and subscription,
ready for the application owner.

87
00:05:42.700 --> 00:05:45.520
Step 5 runs the chapter's tests.

88
00:05:49.270 --> 00:05:54.202
Lab 2 moves to a test subscription with
an Application Gateway WAF version

89
00:05:54.202 --> 00:05:58.657
two and its policy, as the guide's
pilot builds them, never production.

90
00:05:58.657 --> 00:06:03.270
Sign in to commercial Azure, write the
settings file, and run the check live,

91
00:06:03.270 --> 00:06:07.420
allowing the WAF policy queries
that aren't verified on Learn yet.

92
00:06:07.420 --> 00:06:10.812
Then compare with the guide's
Validate query: the mode,

93
00:06:10.812 --> 00:06:14.358
body inspection and rule sets
should match the kit's row.

94
00:06:14.358 --> 00:06:17.219
If a property is named
differently, record it.

95
00:06:17.219 --> 00:06:20.312
Nothing changed in Azure
and the plan wasn't run,

96
00:06:20.312 --> 00:06:22.532
so back out by deleting the output.

97
00:06:22.532 --> 00:06:27.420
The guide's own blocked-request test is
the application owner's, after the switch.

98
00:06:27.420 --> 00:06:29.770
The authors haven't run this lab.

99
00:06:33.520 --> 00:06:37.720
Each output supports a specific control
from the guide's requirements table.

100
00:06:37.720 --> 00:06:39.971
Each policy's mode, body inspection,

101
00:06:39.971 --> 00:06:45.328
rule sets and rate limit support NIST's
information input validation and boundary

102
00:06:45.328 --> 00:06:50.607
protection controls, aligned to a FedRAMP
indicator that cites input validation.

103
00:06:50.607 --> 00:06:55.474
Every internet-facing endpoint behind
a WAF in prevention, the plan reviewed

104
00:06:55.474 --> 00:07:00.570
before it runs, and the logs from every
endpoint all support boundary protection.

105
00:07:00.570 --> 00:07:04.970
The kit's output supports these
controls; the assessor decides.

106
00:07:08.720 --> 00:07:10.983
What breaks, and what guards against it?

107
00:07:10.983 --> 00:07:15.341
A WAF left in detection logs attacks
and lets them reach the backend,

108
00:07:15.341 --> 00:07:18.844
so the finding comes back
every week until it switches.

109
00:07:18.844 --> 00:07:23.738
Automation that switches to prevention
blocks legitimate traffic before tuning,

110
00:07:23.738 --> 00:07:26.007
so the kit only writes the command down.

111
00:07:26.007 --> 00:07:29.668
A Front Door profile with no
security policy has no WAF,

112
00:07:29.668 --> 00:07:34.794
and nothing on the profile says so, so
security policies are read per profile.

113
00:07:34.794 --> 00:07:37.766
And the two services'
property shapes differ,

114
00:07:37.766 --> 00:07:41.010
so each is read on its
own, with a test for both.

115
00:07:44.760 --> 00:07:46.110
Here's what done looks like.

116
00:07:46.110 --> 00:07:50.339
Every internet-facing endpoint
is behind a WAF in prevention,

117
00:07:50.339 --> 00:07:51.998
or has a decision record.

118
00:07:51.998 --> 00:07:56.685
Every policy's rule set is the version
the application owner validated.

119
00:07:56.685 --> 00:08:00.960
The plan file was reviewed with the
application owner before anyone ran it.

120
00:08:00.960 --> 00:08:05.060
And every endpoint's WAF logs
reach the platform workspace.

121
00:08:05.060 --> 00:08:10.970
Next, Chapter 5: a WAF only helps if
nothing can reach the origin around it.
